winewayland: captioned-owned promotion defaults OFF, and never takes modal frames

Shipped ON in 11.10-64 and regressed two things within a day (build tester,
2026-08-27). It was written for ONE extension panel and promoted 929 windows in
a session, because Premiere's VST plugin windows and its dialogs are captioned
owned windows too.

  A. Plugin windows SINK behind the Program Monitor, and menus inside them sink
     behind the plugin. A subsurface is restacked only by us, and nothing
     restacks these -- they are owned windows, so the existing z-order mirror
     (neutron_zorder_collect, which walks GW_CHILD) never sees them, and it only
     matches client_surface anyway.
  B. A file-browse dialog never appears and Premiere WEDGES in its modal loop.

B is fixed here: WS_EX_DLGMODALFRAME separates the failures perfectly. The
tester's census -- 597 promotions carried the bit and every one was a dialog
class, 1302 did not and none was -- and the extension panel does NOT carry it,
so excluding modal frames costs the feature nothing. Exposed as its own gate
(NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_MODAL, default off) purely so the effect can be
isolated on a tester's machine.

A is NOT fixed. The tester established that the plugin windows and the extension
panel are the same window class (st=86cc0000/96cc0000 ex=00000180), so no style
rule can separate them; it needs real z-order propagation into
wl_subsurface.place_above/below. That is a deeper change than belongs in a
same-day hotfix to a path that already documents a FATAL "incorrect sibling"
protocol hazard.

So the default returns to OFF. Docking one panel is a convenience; sinking every
plugin window and freezing on a file dialog is not a trade to make on someone's
working machine. The operator's position was explicit that a trade-off is not an
acceptable outcome -- this is not one, it is a safe default while the real fix
is built.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -316,8 +316,40 @@
  * restriction was really protecting against. Set to 0 for caption-less-only. */
 static BOOL neutron_owned_subsurface_captioned(void)
 {
+    /* ⛔ DEFAULT OFF as of 2026-08-27, one day after it shipped ON in 11.10-64.
+     *
+     * It was written for one extension panel and it promoted 929 windows in a session -- because
+     * Premiere's VST plugin windows and its dialogs are captioned owned windows too. Two severe
+     * regressions followed (build tester, same day):
+     *   A. plugin windows SINK behind the Program Monitor, and menus inside them sink behind the
+     *      plugin. A subsurface is restacked only by us, and nothing was restacking these.
+     *   B. a file-browse dialog never appears and Premiere WEDGES in its modal loop.
+     *
+     * B is fixed below by excluding modal-framed windows. A is NOT fixed: the tester established
+     * that the plugin windows and the extension panel are the SAME window class
+     * (st=86cc0000/96cc0000 ex=00000180), so no style rule can separate them -- it needs real
+     * z-order propagation into wl_subsurface.place_above/below, which is a deeper change than
+     * belongs in a same-day hotfix to a function that already documents a FATAL "incorrect
+     * sibling" protocol hazard.
+     *
+     * So the default goes back to OFF. Docking one panel is a convenience; sinking every plugin
+     * window and freezing on a file dialog is not a trade we get to make on someone's working
+     * machine. Set to 1 to opt in. */
     static int on = -1;
-    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_CAPTIONED"); on = (e && e[0] == '0') ? 0 : 1; }
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_CAPTIONED"); on = (e && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
+    return on;
+}
+
+/* NEUTRON: also promote MODAL-FRAMED owned windows (WS_EX_DLGMODALFRAME). Default OFF -- these
+ * are the ones that wedge Premiere when they become subsurfaces and never present. The build
+ * tester's census separates them perfectly: 597 promotions carried the bit and every one was a
+ * dialog class; 1302 did not and none was. The extension panel this whole line of work exists for
+ * does NOT carry it, so excluding them costs the feature nothing. Exposed as its own gate purely
+ * so the effect can be isolated on a tester's machine. */
+static BOOL neutron_owned_subsurface_modal(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_MODAL"); on = (e && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
     return on;
 }
 
@@ -811,7 +843,12 @@
              * Editor root cause again, one condition away.
              *
              * Kill switch: NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_CAPTIONED=0 restores caption-less-only. */
+            /* WS_EX_DLGMODALFRAME is the discriminator for the freeze (bug B): a modal dialog
+             * that becomes a subsurface and never presents leaves the app in its modal loop
+             * forever. Caption-less windows are unaffected by this test and keep their fix. */
             if (gwowner && gwowner != hwnd && NtUserIsWindowVisible( gwowner ) &&
+                !((NtUserGetWindowLongW( hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE ) & WS_EX_DLGMODALFRAME) &&
+                  !neutron_owned_subsurface_modal()) &&
                 (!(st & WS_CAPTION) || neutron_owned_subsurface_captioned()))
             {
                 ERR( "neutron-ownedsub: hwnd=%p (style=%08x ex=%08x) -> SUBSURFACE of owner=%p "
